window.docContents[158].resolve({"/Hyperbolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Nonlinear-First-Order-Equations--Characteristics-and-Local-Existence":{"contents":"Evans §3.2 (pp. 96–108) derives the characteristic system, compatibility\nand noncharacteristic conditions, local characteristic coordinates, and the\nlocal-existence argument for\n. Here we track how the characteristic flow changes with its\ninitial data. Its first variation satisfies a linear ODE, and estimates for\nthat ODE identify the derivative of the flow. This is the differentiability\nneeded to invert characteristic coordinates and recover a classical solution.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Hyperbolic Equations\u0009Nonlinear First-Order Equations","header":"7.4.1. Characteristics and Local Existence","id":"/Hyperbolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Nonlinear-First-Order-Equations--Characteristics-and-Local-Existence"},"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Poisson___s-energy--Worked-example___-a-Poisson-minimizer-on-an-interval":{"contents":"Take , , and zero boundary values. The candidate\n\n\n\nsatisfies  and . We can check minimization directly,\nwithout guessing the sign of its energy. For a smooth perturbation \nwith , expand the square:\n\n\n\nIntegration by parts gives : the boundary\nterm vanishes and . Consequently,\n\n\n\nThe energy gap is half the squared  norm of the gradient error. If it is zero,  is\nconstant, and its boundary values force . This calculation shows why\nthe weak equation and the boundary condition together yield both minimization\nand uniqueness. The Sobolev theorems below make the same argument without\nassuming smoothness and prove existence when no explicit candidate is known.\n\nThe fluid chapter supplies a useful warning about which energy is being\nminimized. Its rigid rotation\nhas a nonzero velocity gradient but zero symmetric gradient. The\nchecked energy calculation therefore\ngives zero strain energy for that field on every integration domain. This\nis a different functional from the scalar Dirichlet energy : it does\nnot detect rigid rotations. A coercivity argument must account for such\nundetected motions through the admissible class or constraints. Computing an\nintegrand exactly helps identify that issue; compactness and coercivity remain\nanalytic questions.\n\nFixed-point arguments offer another way to construct a solution. Suppose the\nlinear part of a semilinear equation has solution operator  and the\nnonlinearity is . The equation can often be rewritten as\n\n\n\nIf  preserves a nonempty complete metric space and is a contraction there\nwith constant , the theorem of\n gives a unique fixed point. This approach\ndoes not require the nonlinearity to be the derivative of a potential and can\nhandle non-self-adjoint linear terms. Its price is quantitative: the Lipschitz\nconstant of the nonlinearity must be small compared with the coercivity of the\nlinear inverse.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Representation Formulas, Variational Principles, and Fixed Points\u0009Poisson's energy","header":"9.1.1. Worked example: a Poisson minimizer on an interval","id":"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Poisson___s-energy--Worked-example___-a-Poisson-minimizer-on-an-interval"},"/Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives/#PDEAtlas--Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives--Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Sobolev-Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Sobolev___-Morrey___-Poincar______-and-Hardy--Coercivity-and-singular-weights":{"contents":"On every bounded set in positive dimension, Poincaré controls the full\n graph norm by the weak-gradient norm for every finite .\nHere the zero-boundary space is the closure of the compactly supported smooth\ncore; no boundary regularity or extension operator is required. The \ncase supplies the coercivity estimate used by Dirichlet energy methods.\n\nFor every real , Hardy's classical constant\n is proved on finite intervals and on the smooth positive\nhalf-line core.  Division by the boundary coordinate is completed as a\nbounded operator on  for the positive half-line and the flat\nupper half-space. On a bounded operational  domain, the same passage\nfrom the smooth core constructs a bounded linear map\n on .\nIts output agrees almost everywhere with the literal quotient. Combining\nthe chart estimate with Poincaré bounds its  norm by the weak-gradient\nnorm; the resulting domain constant depends on the geometry.\n\nThe iterated quotient by  is completed on\n with the exact odd-weight estimate.  This\nhigher-order branch is one-dimensional and Hilbertian.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Sobolev Spaces and Weak Derivatives\u0009Coverage and Open Problems\u0009Sobolev Coverage and Open Problems\u0009Sobolev, Morrey, Poincaré, and Hardy","header":"4.6.1.6.3. Coercivity and singular weights","id":"/Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives/#PDEAtlas--Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives--Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Sobolev-Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Sobolev___-Morrey___-Poincar______-and-Hardy--Coercivity-and-singular-weights"}});